<h1>Export Labels</h1>
<hr align="left" width="100.526%" size="5" class="whs3">
<p><span class="Command" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Export Labels</b></span> <span lang="EN-US">command is accessible from</span> <b><i style="font-style:normal; font-weight:bold;"><span class="Path" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>File &gt; Export Labels</b></span></i></b> <span lang="EN-US">in</span> <span class="Mode" style="text-decoration:underline;"><u>Inspection</u></span> <span lang="EN-US">mode.</span></p>
<p class="Description"><span class="Command" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Description:</b></span> This command exports all existing labels from the current page in CSV (comma-separated) file format, which can be read by any spreadsheet software (like Microsoft Excel, OpenOffice, etc.) or other statistical software.</p>
<p>To export labels double-click on a page and call <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Export Labels</b></span> command.</p>
<p class="Code">The exported file contains the following information:</p>
<p class="Code"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Page:</b></span> Name_of_page</p>
<p class="Code">Table of labels with the following columns:</p>
<p class="Code"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Name</b></span> - name of the label.<span style="font-weight:bold;"><b><br>
Point X</b></span> - X co-ordinate of the point, to which the label is assigned.<span style="font-weight:bold;"><b><br>
Point Y</b></span> - Y co-ordinate of the point, to which the label is assigned.<span style="font-weight:bold;"><b><br>
Point Z</b></span> - Z co-ordinate of the point, to which the label is assigned.<span style="font-weight:bold;"><b><br>
Reference X</b></span> - X co-ordinate of the closest position from the reference surface.<span style="font-weight:bold;"><b><br>
Reference Y</b></span> - Y co-ordinate of the closest position from the reference surface.<span style="font-weight:bold;"><b><br>
Reference Z</b></span> - Z co-ordinate of the closest position from the reference surface.<span style="font-weight:bold;"><b><br>
Distance X</b></span> - distance from the inspected point to the reference surface along Ox axis.<span style="font-weight:bold;"><b><br>
Distance Y</b></span> - distance from the inspected point to the reference surface along Oy axis.<span style="font-weight:bold;"><b><br>
Distance Z</b></span> - distance from the inspected point to the reference surface along Oz axis.<span style="font-weight:bold;"><b><br>
Distance</b></span> - distance from the inspected point to the reference surface.<span style="font-weight:bold;"><b><br>
Pass/Fail</b></span> - pass/fail criteria. There are two values: PASS - if the distance is within the defined zero range; FAIL - if the distance is outside the defined zero range.</p>
<p>Formatting of the data is set-up from the <span style="font-weight:bold; font-style:italic;"><i><b>System &gt; Preferences &gt; CSV File Export</b></i></span> dialog. KUBE uses the decimal symbol defined in Windows Regional Settings.</p>
